---
import InfoLayout from "../layouts/InfoLayout.astro";
import { CONTACT_RETENTION_DAYS } from "../lib/contact/store";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site";

const sections = [
  { slug: "accounts", text: "Accounts" },
  { slug: "data", text: "Your data" },
  { slug: "tools", text: "Tools" },
  { slug: "games", text: "Games" },
  { slug: "minecraft", text: "Minecraft" },
  { slug: "comments", text: "Comments" },
  { slug: "site", text: "The site" },
] as const;
---

<InfoLayout path="/faq/" sections={sections}>
  <h2 id="accounts">Accounts</h2>
  <h3>Do I need an account?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Reading, the tools and the games all work without one. An account lets
    you comment, keep tool data and game saves across devices, and appear on
    leaderboards.
  </p>
  <h3>How do I sign in?</h3>
  <p>
    With your email address: type it on the <a href="/sign-in">sign-in page</a>
    and the site emails you a code. There’s no password, so there’s none to
    forget or leak. A new address makes an account the same way. You can also
    sign in with GitHub, and link GitHub to an account you made by email from
    your <a href="/account/security/">security page</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>I used to sign in with GitHub. What changed?</h3>
  <p>
    Nothing you need to do much about. Your account, username and everything in
    it stay; signing in with GitHub still works. Accounts are now known by their
    email address, so the first time you sign in, your account takes the address
    GitHub has verified for you (or asks for one). Renaming yourself on GitHub
    no longer changes anything here.
  </p>
  <h3>What’s a passkey?</h3>
  <p>
    A way to sign in with your fingerprint, face or screen lock instead of an
    email code. Add one on your <a href="/account/security/">security page</a>;
    your device or password manager keeps it, and the site only keeps its public
    half.
  </p>
  <h3>Will I know if someone else signs in?</h3>
  <p>
    If your account is signed in to from a browser it hasn’t used lately, you
    get an email (you can switch these off, though it’s better not to). Your
    security page also lists recent sign-ins. And if your address is changed,
    the old one gets a link that undoes it.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I change my username?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, on your <a href="/account/profile/">profile page</a>, once a month.
    Links to your old username keep working for a while.
  </p>
  <h3>How old do I need to be?</h3>
  <p>At least 14 to make an account. Anyone can use the site signed out.</p>
  <h3>How do I delete my account?</h3>
  <p>
    From <a href="/account/">your account</a>: “Delete account” removes your
    profile, comments, tool data, game saves, files, Minecraft projects and
    email address. Download your data first if you want to keep it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data">Your data</h2>
  <h3>What do you know about me?</h3>
  <p>
    If you’ve never signed in: nothing. If you have: your{" "}
    <a href="/account/export.json">data export</a> lists all of it. The{" "}
    <a href="/legal/privacy/">privacy policy</a> explains each part.
  </p>
  <h3>Is there tracking or analytics?</h3>
  <p>
    No tracking and no ads. Page views are counted by Cloudflare Web Analytics,
    which uses no cookies and doesn’t identify you. The site sets one cookie,
    only once you sign in. See <a href="/legal/cookies/">cookies</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>What happens to a message I send through the contact form?</h3>
  <p>
    Only I read it, and it’s deleted within {CONTACT_RETENTION_DAYS} days. If
    you leave an email address, it’s used only to reply.
  </p>
  <h3>Will the site email me?</h3>
  <p>
    Only if you add an address on your{" "}
    <a href="/account/email/">account’s email page</a> and confirm it with the
    code it sends. Then you get the notifications you leave on (replies to your
    comments, decisions about your uploads and projects), never newsletters.
    Every email has an unsubscribe link, and you can remove the address at any
    time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tools">Tools</h2>
  <h3>Are my files uploaded when I convert them?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Convert, File info, QR and Color run entirely in your browser; the files
    never leave your device. That’s also why some formats depend on what your
    browser supports.
  </p>
  <h3>Where are my notes kept?</h3>
  <p>
    Signed out, in this browser only. Signed in, in your account, so they follow
    you. When you first sign in, Notes offers to move what’s in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2 id="games">Games</h2>
  <h3>Can I lose my Skygrid island?</h3>
  <p>
    Signed out, it lives in your browser: clearing site data deletes it. Sign in
    and it’s saved to your account, checked by the server as you play.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I sell coins or items?</h3>
  <p>
    Only in the game’s Bazaar, for other in-game things. Trading them for real
    money isn’t allowed (see the <a href="/legal/terms/#games">terms</a>).
  </p>
  <h3>I found a bug or an exploit.</h3>
  <p>
    Thank you: please <a href="/contact/">report it</a> instead of using it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="minecraft">Minecraft</h2>
  <h3>How do I share a creation?</h3>
  <p>
    Sign in, open <a href="/account/minecraft/">your projects</a> and make a new
    one: a page with pictures, then a release with its file. Drop the file in
    and most of the form fills itself in. Others see it once the release is
    published.
  </p>
  <h3>Why is my mod still waiting?</h3>
  <p>
    Mods and plugins run code on people’s computers, so every one is looked at
    by a person first. Other files wait only until you’re trusted (see the{" "}
    <a href="/legal/guidelines/#uploads">guidelines</a>).
  </p>
  <h3>Is this an official Minecraft site?</h3>
  <p>
    No. {SITE_NAME} is not an official Minecraft product, and is not approved by
    or associated with Mojang or Microsoft.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comments">Comments</h2>
  <h3>Why hasn’t my comment appeared?</h3>
  <p>
    Your first comment waits until it’s read. After one is approved, the rest
    appear straight away. You can see your waiting comments under the post.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I comment under a different name?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes: set a display name in <a href="/account/profile/">your profile</a>, or
    choose to show only your username.
  </p>

  <h2 id="site">The site</h2>
  <h3>Who runs {SITE_NAME}?</h3>
  <p>
    Trilleo, one person. See <a href="/about/">About</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>How is it built?</h3>
  <p>
    Astro, React and Postgres on a small server; the{" "}
    <a href="/colophon/">colophon</a> has the details and credits.
  </p>
  <h3>Is there a feed?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes: <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>, with every post in full.
  </p>
  <h3>My question isn’t here.</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/contact/">Ask it</a>.
  </p>
</InfoLayout>
